WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.830
Joshua Childress is a 37 year old father

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:06.960
of two who spent seven years as a U.S.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:09.450
Border Patrol agent out of Arizona's

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.969
Yuma Station tracking footprints to

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:14.849
catch illegal crossers he resigned last

00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:14.349
month after changing his mind about

00:00:14.349 --> 00:00:14.849
illegal immigration.

00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:20.939
I went in because I

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:25.410
have a background in the military it was

00:00:20.939 --> 00:00:27.570
something I was good at, and it seemed

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:29.369
like something that needed done.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:33.329
My understanding of the laws at the time

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:36.390
was that there were proper ways to get

00:00:33.329 --> 00:00:38.480
into this country legally and that the

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:40.260
people that were coming across were just

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.540
shirking those laws

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.239
What is the border situation there?

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.129
Is there a fence or a wall?

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:49.649
What kind of obstacles are people trying to get around?

00:00:47.129 --> 00:00:52.260
Most of our area does have

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:54.930
fencing infrastructure. There are parts

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.430
that have barriers that just will stop vehicles.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:54.930
How did people get past that?

00:00:54.930 --> 00:01:00.899
Oh, they would just climb over.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:03.989
Would a wall

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:06.750
going across the entire border be useful

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:08.430
and stop people from coming over?

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:12.900
I don't think so it's been my experience that

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:15.360
whatever we do they find a way to adapt

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.369
and will adapt to that and then they

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.200
adapt to that. I mean it's a it seems to

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:21.960
be a pretty constant back and forth.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.880
Border Patrol's policy of separating

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.070
parents and children crossing the

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.729
southern border sparked a national

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:31.650
outcry this summer which Childress says

00:01:28.729 --> 00:01:33.930
underscores the impact the press and the

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:36.770
public can have on policy.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:40.439
So there were separations under Obama.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:41.220
No, it wasn't the the official policy to use it as

00:01:40.439 --> 00:01:44.189
punishment.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:48.240
Obviously Trump did try to use that

00:01:44.189 --> 00:01:51.990
policy. It was pretty short-lived as we

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:54.119
saw it. When I left, the grounds for

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:57.000
separating anybody were more relaxed

00:01:54.119 --> 00:02:00.420
than they were under Obama, so that's the

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:03.509
effect that the public outrage, I think,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.670
has had, is that when the people just

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:04.170
won't tolerate it

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:04.670
it's going to have to change.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:10.470
Were there

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:12.880
any particular experiences that made you

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:16.210
start second-guessing this

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.710
entire endeavor?

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:16.210
Not actually an incident

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:20.500
where I caught somebody, but after

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:22.660
they're caught they have to be processed

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:27.310
and I was assigned to processing one day

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:27.970
and as a routine part of the questioning

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:31.540
we have to ask

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:34.330
if they have scars or tattoos, and this guy

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:36.880
said he had scars and when he lifted up

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:39.160
his shirt to show me the scars there

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.140
were lash marks all over his back. When I

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:45.640
asked him what those were from he

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:48.160
explained that he refused to carry drugs

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:52.930
for a smuggler and they tied him up and

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:54.970
whipped him for it I didn't feel good

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:57.940
about sending that guy back and that was

00:02:54.970 --> 00:03:00.070
a dramatic example but there's countless

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:02.710
others that didn't have a dramatic story

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:02.210
like that that they just want a better

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:02.710
life and I stopped being able to

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:07.090
reconcile that.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.400
What also changed his

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:11.380
mind was listening to podcasts while

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.950
driving around the border 8 to 10 hours

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:16.810
a day. Specifically a show called

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:20.050
Unregistered, hosted by historian and

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:22.420
recent contributor Thaddeus Russell.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:23.200
One particular episode featuring author and

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:26.170
sex worker

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.670
Maggie MacNeil made him reexamine his

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:26.170
fundamental beliefs.

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:31.270
And I've actually

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:32.770
had people go, "Oh yeah, I'm a whore!" - I'll go

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:35.230
"No no no, I mean an actual prostitute

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:38.460
as in actually take money for

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:41.320
sex" and then they'll be like "oh oh oh"

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:46.270
because they're thinking I mean slut

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:48.850
How did the ideas being

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:51.550
expressed there changed the way you

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:56.230
started to think about your job?

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:58.750
So there were two guests of Thad's that when I

00:03:56.230 --> 00:04:01.150
first saw them I wasn't completely

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:02.620
thrilled. I didn't really want to hear

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:05.110
what they had to say, I thought, and one

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:10.000
of those is Maggie MacNeil and Conner

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:12.550
Habib who's a gay porn star. I don't have

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.800
anything morally against either those

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:19.080
lifestyles but I also didn't really want

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:22.479
to hear about it. But once I finally did

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:26.080
download and listen to those episodes, I

00:04:22.479 --> 00:04:26.740
was blown away. I mean, they had so much

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.720
more to offer

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.699
and so much more to say than these

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:33.490
little categories that we put them in as

00:04:30.699 --> 00:04:35.560
a pornstar or prostitute. They're

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:37.120
fascinating people: They're brave. They're

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:40.030
smart. They're heroic.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:43.720
This small bias in my mind almost

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:47.560
stopped me from listening to these

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:51.490
people's stories and how much enrichment

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:55.180
I got from listening to their stories, so

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:57.990
then it made me start to take stock of

00:04:55.180 --> 00:05:00.310
the rest of my life. If I had these

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:02.860
biases that I didn't really realize I

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:04.509
had, what else is there? How do I really?

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:06.940
feel about the drug war? How do I really

00:05:04.509 --> 00:05:09.370
feel about prisons? And immigration

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:11.560
naturally came up as one of those

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:13.150
opinions.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.860
And so what are your views about immigration now?

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:17.470
I think we're moving in the wrong direction. I think

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:20.050
we're moving towards more restriction

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:22.210
more enforcement and I think there

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:24.699
should be less. As Bryan Caplan, the

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:28.330
economist, says, if there's not a good

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:30.370
reason to keep you out, come on in. He

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:33.180
basically says that if there's no

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:35.440
communicable disease, no violent past,

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:37.599
there's no reason that you shouldn't be

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.719
allowed to come in and try to make a

00:05:37.599 --> 00:05:44.710
life for yourself. I want to see a freer

00:05:40.719 --> 00:05:46.719
world I want people to take charge of

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:49.180
their lives. I want people to be able to

00:05:46.719 --> 00:05:54.099
make their own choices, and I don't want

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:56.620
an imaginary boundary to get in the way

00:05:54.099 --> 00:06:00.810
of that. They're being spoken about as if

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:04.569
they are pieces to be moved around or

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:07.360
assets to be utilized. Like, no, they're people.

00:06:04.569 --> 00:06:10.000
Deport all criminal aliens and

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:13.419
dismantle every last criminal gang and

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:16.300
cartel threatening our citizens.

00:06:13.419 --> 00:06:18.849
We're told from the Trump administration that

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:21.930
the reason that we need to get tough on

00:06:18.849 --> 00:06:24.539
the border is to stop, you know, ms-13

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:27.310
members from coming over, criminals,

00:06:24.539 --> 00:06:29.319
violent criminals and drug traffickers

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:33.069
and child traffickers.

00:06:29.319 --> 00:06:37.150
Those people do exist, and we do come across them, but

00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:39.669
I no longer believe that immigration

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:40.760
policy or immigration restriction is the

00:06:39.669 --> 00:06:43.130
solution.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:46.160
If you have problems with

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:48.920
drug trafficking and violent gangs, which

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:50.200
are also linked to drugs most of the

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:54.680
time,

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:59.060
well let's legalize drugs in the United

00:06:54.680 --> 00:07:01.070
States and that takes a significant

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:05.090
amount of power out of those guy's hands.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:07.490
We can't fix the problem just by more

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:09.710
restriction.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:11.600
Childress recently appeared on Russell's podcast for a long-form

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:13.940
discussion about his intellectual

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:15.080
journey and ultimate decision to leave

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:17.330
the Border Patrol.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:19.580
He's now working on a maintenance crew

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:24.740
at an apartment complex alongside

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:28.490
several Mexican immigrants.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:34.430
If what I have to say has any effect I hope that

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:36.320
it is that people will take inventory of

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:38.480
their life and take inventory of their

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:43.580
views. Think about why you believe what

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:46.880
you believe, and if you can go through it

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:49.340
and think, "yeah these beliefs are firmly

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:50.630
rooted," good. That's gonna help you sleep

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:53.420
at night that's gonna help you lead a

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:55.910
peaceful life, but if it's not I think

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:59.009
that's valuable information to have

